#!/bin/bash
# Plex HTPC launch wrapper — see KNOWN_BUGS.md NVIDIA-002
#
# Shadows /usr/bin/Plex (symlink to /opt/plex-htpc/Plex.sh). ~/.local/bin is
# first in both the interactive PATH and the systemd user environment, so this
# catches app-menu launches (KDE starts it as app-tv.plex.PlexHTPC@*.service,
# whose Exec=Plex resolves through that PATH) as well as CLI launches.
#
# The bug: QtWebEngine's Chromium GPU process cannot create an EGL context on
# the NVIDIA driver --
#
#   eglCreateContext failed with error EGL_BAD_CONTEXT
#   ContextResult::kFatalFailure: Failed to create shared context for virtualization
#   GpuChannel: Failed to create SharedImageStub
#
# The app still takes its display fullscreen and then renders nothing, so the
# monitor reads as frozen and unresponsive rather than as a crashed app. The
# failure also retries in a tight loop — a 15 second run produced 745,132 lines
# of it, which floods the journal.
#
# Not XWayland-specific: reproduced identically with DISABLE_WAYLAND=0, so the
# native Wayland path is no escape. The same fault hits Jellyfin Media Player,
# which shares Plex Media Player's codebase.
#
# --disable-gpu alone is NOT enough. It silences the EGL errors, but the window
# then renders as black/see-through with only the titlebar drawn: Chromium is
# software-rasterising while the Qt Quick scene graph is still on OpenGL, and the
# web view texture never composites into it. Both halves have to be software
# before the UI draws. Verified by screenshot — with both set, the Plex sign-in
# screen renders correctly.
#
# Things that do NOT fix it, all confirmed by measurement here:
#   DISABLE_WAYLAND=0 (native Wayland)                  -> still fails
#   __EGL_VENDOR_LIBRARY_FILENAMES=10_nvidia.json       -> 1,057,736 errors
#   QTWEBENGINE_DISABLE_SANDBOX=1                       -> 2,094,720 errors
# The box has an RTX 5090 and an AMD Granite Ridge iGPU, so wrong-GPU selection
# was the obvious theory; pinning EGL to NVIDIA alone disproved it.
#
# KNOWN LIMITATION: under a software scene graph, mpv (vo=libmpv, which renders
# into that scene graph) did not create its GL render context — no
# "libmpv_render: GL_VERSION" line appears, unlike every GPU-path run. Video
# playback is therefore UNVERIFIED and may not work. Verifying it needs a signed-in
# Plex session. If video is broken, prefer `plezy` (in cachyos-extra): GTK + mpv
# with no Chromium anywhere, so this whole bug class does not apply.
#
# Re-test on NVIDIA driver upgrades; drop this wrapper if upstream fixes it.
export QTWEBENGINE_CHROMIUM_FLAGS="--disable-gpu${QTWEBENGINE_CHROMIUM_FLAGS:+ ${QTWEBENGINE_CHROMIUM_FLAGS}}"
export QT_QUICK_BACKEND="${QT_QUICK_BACKEND:-software}"

exec /opt/plex-htpc/Plex.sh "$@"
